libc++abi: export the emulated TLS runtime on Darwin when built with GCC

GCC implements thread_local with emulated TLS on Darwin, and since GCC 14
it reports __has_feature(cxx_thread_local), so libc++abi keeps its
exception globals in a thread_local and needs ___emutls_get_address.
The GCC driver normally supplies it from libgcc, but not with
-nodefaultlibs, as used for a runtime in /usr/lib.

Clang-built code on macOS 10.6 and earlier needs the same symbol from
libc++: dyld has no thread-local variable support before 10.7, so
MacPorts' Clang uses emulated TLS there and leaves the runtime to
libc++. MacPorts' libc++ 5.0.1 (+emulated_tls, a default variant on
those systems) built compiler-rt's emutls.c into libc++abi, re-exported
by libc++, and binaries built that way bind ___emutls_get_address to
/usr/lib/libc++.1.dylib. Prebuilt Arctic Fox for 10.6 is one: of the 91
symbols it imports from libc++, this is the only one which upstream
libc++ does not export.

Link GCC's own copy, libemutls_w.a, into libc++abi and export (and
re-export) ___emutls_get_address through a dedicated symbol list; -u
makes the linker load the archive member whatever libc++abi itself
references. libgcc builds this copy with weak definitions and default
visibility, so that dyld merges all copies in a process (libgcc_s, other
images linking libemutls_w.a, this one) into one; compiler-rt's would be
a separate, strong implementation. Both lay out the per-variable control
object the same way (size, alignment, index, template), so Clang-built
code works with GCC's runtime.

When libc++ is built on another ABI library (libsupc++ or libstdc++)
there is no libc++abi to carry it, so libc++ links it itself; libc++ has
no export list, so the symbol is exported there as well.

diff --git a/libcxx/src/CMakeLists.txt b/libcxx/src/CMakeLists.txt
--- a/libcxx/src/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/libcxx/src/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -208,6 +208,11 @@
     target_link_libraries(cxx_shared PUBLIC libcxx-abi-shared)
   endif()
 
+  # libc++abi provides GCC's emulated TLS runtime; without it, libc++ does.
+  if (APPLE AND CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER_ID STREQUAL "GNU" AND NOT LIBCXX_CXX_ABI MATCHES "libcxxabi$")
+    target_link_libraries(cxx_shared PRIVATE "-Wl,-u,___emutls_get_address" emutls_w)
+  endif()
+
   # Maybe force some symbols to be weak, not weak or not exported.
   # TODO: This shouldn't depend on the platform, and ideally it should be done in the sources.
   # Old ld64 versions do not understand these options (see DarwinLinkerFeatures.cmake).

diff --git a/libcxxabi/lib/emutls.exp b/libcxxabi/lib/emutls.exp
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/libcxxabi/lib/emutls.exp
@@ -0,0 +1,4 @@
+# Emulated TLS runtime (GCC's libemutls_w.a). libc++abi uses it itself when
+# built with GCC, and Clang-built code on macOS 10.6 and earlier expects
+# libc++ to provide it.
+___emutls_get_address

diff --git a/libcxxabi/src/CMakeLists.txt b/libcxxabi/src/CMakeLists.txt
--- a/libcxxabi/src/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/libcxxabi/src/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -284,6 +284,11 @@
     reexport_symbols("${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/../lib/cxa-thread-atexit.exp")
   endif()
 
+  if (APPLE AND CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER_ID STREQUAL "GNU")
+    target_link_libraries(cxxabi_shared PRIVATE "-Wl,-u,___emutls_get_address" emutls_w)
+    reexport_symbols("${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/../lib/emutls.exp")
+  endif()
+
   if (LIBCXXABI_ENABLE_NEW_DELETE_DEFINITIONS)
     reexport_symbols("${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/../lib/new-delete.exp")
   endif()
